---
import "../styles/surfaces.css";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadBenchmarksPage } from "../lib/surfaces";
import { loadPublishedPaint } from "../data/published-paint";

const page = loadBenchmarksPage();
const paint = loadPublishedPaint();
const h = page.headline;
const description =
  "Published ChartKit benchmark numbers: two-rAF paint, bundle size, browser render, server render, sampling, and the agent eval.";
---

<Base title="Benchmarks" description={description} tone="light">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" href="/benchmarks.md" />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap surface">
    <header class="hero">
      <p class="kicker">Benchmarks</p>
      <h1>Benchmarks</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Round {page.round}, measured {page.measured} on phase 2 <code>{page.coreCommit}</code>. Five browser samples,
        Playwright Chromium, a 640×360 line, three x spacings. This page does not re-time the libraries. The same
        numbers are in <a href="/benchmarks.json">benchmarks.json</a> (schema{" "}
        <a href="/benchmarks.schema.json">benchmarks.schema.json</a>) and <a href="/benchmarks.md">benchmarks.md</a>.
        The agent eval was not re-run; it stays {page.agentMeasured}.
      </p>
    </header>
    <p>{page.updateMethod}</p>

    <h2 id="paint">Paint</h2>
    <p>{paint.caption}</p>
    {paint.notes.map((note) => <p>{note}</p>)}
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="paint-matrix">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Chart</th>
            <th class="num">Points</th>
            {paint.libraries.map((library) => <th class="num">{library}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {paint.rows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.label}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.pointsLabel}</td>
              {row.cells.map((cell) => (
                <td
                  class:list={[
                    "num",
                    cell.state === "ok" && cell.library === "ChartKit" && "is-us",
                    cell.state === "dnf" && "is-dnf",
                    cell.state === "na" && "is-na",
                    cell.state === "absent" && "is-absent",
                  ]}
                  title={cell.title}
                >
                  {cell.text}
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Bundle size</h2>
    <p>
      ChartKit’s line entry is {h.lineGzipBytes} bytes gzip ({h.lineGzipLabel}), from <code>scripts/size.mjs</code> on{" "}
      <code>{h.sizeCommit}</code>, measured {h.sizeMeasured}. {h.bundleLoss} {h.lineWins} The harness minimal line on
      that run is {h.harnessLineBytes} bytes. {h.donutWin}
    </p>
    <div class="ck-frame" role="img" aria-label={page.bundle.alt} set:html={page.bundle.svg} />
    <p class="board-meta">{page.bundle.caption}</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Library</th>
            <th class="num">gzip bytes</th>
            <th>Note</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.bundleRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.lib}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.gzip}</td>
              <td>{row.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Browser render</h2>
    <p>
      ChartKit wins the initial render in all 12 cells against uPlot. Even 1,000,000 points is 23.6 ms vs 26.5 ms. The
      aligned update wins 6 of 12 and loses 6. The losses are in the table, not a footnote: even 1k (1.5 vs 1.2), even
      10k (1.9 vs 1.7), jittered 1k (1.6 vs 1.4), and gapped timestamps at 1k (1.6 vs 1.4), 10k (1.9 vs 1.6), and 100k
      (3.5 vs 3.4).
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <article class="card">
        <div class="ck-frame" role="img" aria-label={page.initial1k.alt} set:html={page.initial1k.svg} />
        <p>{page.initial1k.caption}</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <div class="ck-frame" role="img" aria-label={page.initial100k.alt} set:html={page.initial100k.svg} />
        <p>{page.initial100k.caption}</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <article class="card">
      <div class="ck-frame" role="img" aria-label={page.initial1m.alt} set:html={page.initial1m.svg} />
      <p>{page.initial1m.caption}</p>
    </article>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>X spacing</th>
            <th>Points</th>
            <th class="num">ChartKit initial</th>
            <th class="num">uPlot initial</th>
            <th class="num">ChartKit update</th>
            <th class="num">uPlot update</th>
            <th>Call</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.browserRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.shape}</td>
              <td>{row.points}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.ckI}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.upI}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.ckU}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.upU}</td>
              <td class={row.tone === "loss" ? "loss" : undefined}>{row.call}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In these 12 cells ChartKit is also faster than Chart.js and Observable Plot on both the initial and the aligned
      update. Even 1,000,000 points: Chart.js updates in 189.8 ms and Plot in 1,295.8 ms. ECharts, Recharts, and Nivo
      are not in the round 13 browser rows, so this page does not give them a browser millisecond.
    </p>

    <h2>Canvas line</h2>
    <p>
      Measured {page.canvas.measured}, median of 5, Playwright Chromium, 640×360, sampling auto. This is not a re-time
      of round {page.round}. Sampling none was not re-timed.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Points</th>
            {page.canvas.heads.map((head) => <th class="num">{head}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.canvas.rows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.points}</td>
              {row.cells.map((cell) => <td class="num">{cell}</td>)}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Canvas loses first paint at every size: {page.canvas.losses}.</p>

    <h2>Hover</h2>
    <p>
      Steady hover at 100,000 points is close. ChartKit’s painted median is 16.5 ms and uPlot’s is 16.2 ms. First hover
      on a fresh chart is not close: uPlot paints in 4 to 4.9 ms, and ChartKit paints in 15.9 to 20.8 ms.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Library</th>
            <th class="num">Tip ms</th>
            <th class="num">Painted median ms</th>
            <th class="num">Painted p95 ms</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.hoverRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.lib}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.tip}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.painted}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.p95}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>First hover</th>
            <th class="num">ChartKit painted</th>
            <th class="num">ChartKit max</th>
            <th class="num">uPlot painted</th>
            <th class="num">uPlot max</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.firstHoverRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.delay}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.ck}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.ckMax}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.uplot}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.uplotMax}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Downsampling</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>The fast large-series times use sampling.</strong>
      <code>mark.sampling</code> defaults to <code>auto</code>, which minmax-downsamples a line once it is longer than
      twice the plot width. 1,000 points stay complete. 10k, 100k, and 1M do not. With sampling off, ChartKit draws
      every point:
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Points, sampling none</th>
            <th class="num">Initial ms</th>
            <th class="num">Update ms</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.samplingRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.points}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.initial}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.update}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      These sampling-none times use the same fair update timer. At 1,000,000 points that is 471.8 ms to draw and 462.4
      ms to update, not the old two-frame figure.
    </p>

    <h2>Server render</h2>
    <div class="ck-frame" role="img" aria-label={page.ssr.alt} set:html={page.ssr.svg} />
    <p class="board-meta">{page.ssr.caption}</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Library</th>
            <th class="num">Mean ms</th>
            <th class="num">Median ms</th>
            <th>Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.ssrRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.lib}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.mean}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.median}</td>
              <td>{row.status}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="agents">Agent eval</h2>
    <p>
      Round 6, recorded in BENCHMARKS.md. Not re-run for round {page.round}. One model, {page.published.model}, temperature{" "}
      {page.published.temperature}, one run on {page.agentMeasured}, {page.published.tasks} tasks. “Official” means the
      library’s docs were in the prompt. ChartKit’s docs are <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>. The previous file, the
      React-only v0 text, scored 18/42 in an earlier run.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Library</th>
            <th>No docs, first</th>
            <th>No docs, within two</th>
            <th>Official, first</th>
            <th>Official, within two</th>
            <th>Mean first-try tokens, no docs / official</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {page.agentRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.lib}</td>
              <td>{row.noFirst}</td>
              <td>{row.noTwo}</td>
              <td>{row.offFirst}</td>
              <td>{row.offTwo}</td>
              <td>{row.tokens}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      ChartKit’s first try with no docs is {h.repairFrom}. Recharts, ECharts, and Chart.js were 42/42 with no docs.{" "}
      <code>repair()</code>, with no model call, takes that ChartKit output to {h.repairTo}. With the official docs,
      ChartKit’s first try is already 42/42, at a mean of {page.published.tokensOfficial[0]} output tokens. Recharts
      with its docs used a mean of {page.published.tokensOfficial[1]}, ECharts {page.published.tokensOfficial[2]}, and
      Chart.js {page.published.tokensOfficial[3]}. The other libraries have no repair step. Phase 1 grew llms.txt to
      8,274 bytes, about 1.9 KB more than the competitor excerpts. A ChartKit-only rerun with the file cut to 6,329
      bytes scored 41/42 on the first try. The miss was a combo axis, CK3002, which <code>repair()</code> does not
      patch. The headline numbers use the full file. <a href="/agents">How to use the loop</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
